// ATR trailing stop — ride the trend, exit mechanically
//
// Enter long when price closes above its 50-bar average, then protect the position with a stop set
// a multiple of ATR below the close and RE-ARMED every bar. The stop only ever tightens in a rising
// market because it trails the close; it never widens on the way down, because the exit is taken
// the moment the level is touched.
//
// This is the asymmetry the Covenant is built around (BLUEPRINT Part V): small mechanical losses,
// occasional large riding wins. Humans invert it reliably — they take profits like thieves and hold
// losses like heirlooms. The machine does not hope.
//
// WHAT THE BACKTEST WILL AND WILL NOT TELL YOU. Stops fill intra-bar here, which is honest, but two
// costs are modelled deliberately pessimistically because the alternative flatters: a bar that GAPS
// through the stop fills at the open, not at the stop; and when a bar touches both the stop and a
// target, the stop is taken, because which came first is unknowable without tick data.
//@version=1
strategy("ATR trailing stop")

trend = sma(close, 50)
atr14 = atr(14)

if close > trend and strategy.position_size == 0
    strategy.entry("L", strategy.long)

// Re-armed every bar: this is what makes it TRAIL. A stop set once at entry is a fixed stop.
strategy.exit("L", close - 3 * atr14, na)

// Stand down when the trend breaks, rather than waiting to be stopped out of a position the regime
// no longer supports. Cash is a position (Covenant 4).
if close < trend
    strategy.close("L")

plot(trend)
